 "limitation": "POSITION-AS-ASPECT PROXY: the 665 m global DEM cannot resolve hollow-scale (sub-km) wall slopes, so a hollow's azimuthal position on the host-crater wall is used as a proxy for the aspect (facing direction) of the scarp it occupies (wall at azimuth A faces A+180). Host assignment uses the SMALLEST Herrick crater whose 1.1 crater-radii contain the hollow, which can attach a wall hollow to a small superposed crater. A detection/illumination bias (equator-facing = better-lit walls easier to detect) could inflate the poleward-side count; the floor control bounds this.",
 "interpretation": "Weak but strongly latitude-structured support for an insolation-driven, equator-facing aspect preference in the north. Pooled northern wall hollows show frac_poleward=0.524 (n=2151, binomial p=0.025), but the per-crater bootstrap 0.550 [0.495,0.604] includes 0.5, so the pooled signal is not robust to weighting craters equally. The robust result is the band structure in frac_poleward and the V-test: the preference is absent at 0-15N (0.48) and REVERSED (pole-facing) at 15-30N (0.39, p~9e-11), then becomes strong and increases with latitude at 30-45N (0.62), 45-60N (0.71) and 60-75N (0.75), each binomial p<1e-3 with V-test toward the equator-facing direction p<1e-6. An aspect control that grows toward the pole is the expected signature of insolation asymmetry between equator- and pole-facing slopes. The 15-30N reversal is anomalous and may reflect the 90-150E longitude province or a real transition. CIRCULAR MEDIAN CAVEAT: the pooled northern median facing aspect (191 deg) is numerically close to Thomas et al. 2014 (187 deg), but the pooled distribution is near-uniform (Rbar=0.025) so its bootstrap CI spans nearly the whole circle and the value is not meaningful. Among the three bands with a detected preference, only 30-45N has a circular median facing aspect in the equatorward range (205.6 deg, close to 187); at 45-60N (110.7 deg) and 60-75N (159.8 deg) the circular median does NOT fall near 180 deg because the facing-aspect distributions are broad and skewed (medoid median unstable). In these bands the equator-facing concentration is established by frac_poleward (0.71, 0.75) and the V-test (p<1e-6), NOT by the circular median. So Thomas 2014 is reproduced cleanly by the median only at 30-45N; the equator-facing preference itself (not the exact median) holds across 30-75N. The southern sample (n=250) is dominated by 0-15S (n=182) and shows no equator-facing preference (0.468, ns; 0-15S is 0.43, marginally pole-facing); because the south lacks mid/high-latitude wall hollows the hemispheric contrast is largely a latitude-sampling difference rather than a demonstrated asymmetry. FLOOR CONTROL: per-crater frac_poleward is ~0.50 (0.500 all, 0.522 N), i.e. no preference when field-weighted, as expected for a control; the raw floor-N binomial (0.557, p<1e-7) is inflated by a few large fields, a caution that raw binomials overstate significance here. PEAK/CENTRAL hollows (rho<0.3, D>100 km, n=399): per-crater 0.498, no preference. HOT vs WARM longitudes: muddy; N-warm shows a raw equator-facing excess (0.551) but per-crater 0.482, and the effect tracks the 90-150E province at 40-70N rather than a clean insolation-longitude split.",
 "verdict": "Latitude-structured, insolation-consistent equator-facing preference confirmed at mid-to-high northern latitudes (30-75N, frac_poleward 0.62-0.75, V-test toward equator p<1e-6); absent at the northern equator and reversed at 15-30N; not detectable in the small, low-latitude-dominated southern sample. The circular median facing aspect reproduces Thomas et al. 2014 (187 deg) cleanly only at 30-45N (205.6 deg); at 45-60N and 60-75N the distributions are too broad for a stable median (110.7, 159.8 deg), so those bands support the equator-facing preference via frac_poleward and the V-test but not via the median value. Floor and peak controls show no preference under per-crater weighting, supporting a genuine wall-aspect effect. PROVISIONAL: rests on a position-as-aspect proxy (665 m DEM cannot resolve hollow-scale slopes)."
}